    WHAT 6.4-2 SHIPS
    
    A. Registry substrate (`ql-functions/src/registry.rs`)
    
      1. New `udf_handles: HashMap<String, FunctionImplHandle>` field on
         `FunctionRegistry`, parallel to `metadata`. Stores the opaque dispatch
         pointer the 6.4-3 worker dispatcher will consult; populated only for
         UDFs (built-ins have no handle, their dispatch goes through `fns`).
    
      2. New `register_udf(meta, handle)` combined method. Calls
         `register_metadata` first (which can fail Conflict); on success inserts
         the handle. Single `fn_gen` bump (inside `register_metadata`). The
         Conflict short-circuits BEFORE the handle insert → no partial state
         (the inverse — metadata without handle — is the normal state for
         built-ins and metadata-only test registrations).
    
      3. New `udf_handle(canonical_name) -> Option<FunctionImplHandle>` reader.
         Case-insensitive (uppercases the query).
    
      4. `unregister_metadata` extended to ALSO remove the matching
         `udf_handles` entry symmetrically on success. The builtin-guard at the
         top of the function ensures UDF-only targeting, so the handle removal
         is naturally UDF-only. Closes the invariant "every key in
         `udf_handles` has a matching `metadata` entry" intact.
    
      5. `use ql_session::session::FunctionImplHandle` added.
    
      Tests (+4 in ql-functions::registry::tests):
      - `register_udf_inserts_metadata_and_handle_atomically` — happy path +
        fn_gen single-bump.
      - `udf_handle_returns_none_for_builtins_and_unknown_names` — pins the
        invariant.
      - `unregister_metadata_clears_udf_handle_symmetrically` — round-trip.
      - `register_udf_conflict_does_not_insert_handle` — atomicity property.
    
    B. Engine trait wiring (`ql-exec/src/session.rs`)
    
      1. `WorkbookSession::register_function` — replaces the
         `not_implemented_in_v1_core` stub. `ensure_ready` lifecycle gate +
         `Arc::make_mut(&mut self.registry).register_udf(meta, handle)?` +
         `self.graph.on_function_registered(&canonical_name)` for dirty
         transitive-fan. Wrapped in a `FaultGuard` (6.1C audit-fix M3 pattern)
         so a panic between the registry and graph mutations seals the session
         `Faulted` rather than leaves torn state. Disarms on the Conflict-Err
         path so legitimate Conflict does NOT fault the session.
    
      2. `WorkbookSession::unregister_function` — symmetric.
         `unregister_metadata?` (which symmetrically clears the udf_handles
