    DESIGN CALLS (locked + documented in source)
    
    1. UDF handle on `FunctionRegistry` (not on `WorkbookSession`) — the
       registry IS the authority on function identity; 6.4-3's worker
       dispatcher will look up `registry.udf_handle(name)` to invoke the
       Python worker.
    
    2. `register_udf` combined method (not separate `set_metadata` +
       `set_handle` calls) — atomic, single fn_gen bump, no two-phase commit
       awkwardness. `register_metadata` stays for built-in boot + metadata-
       only tests.
    
    3. `Arc::make_mut(&mut self.registry)` is O(1) here because strong-count
       is 1 in the normal flow (the open/import `Arc::clone`-then-drop pattern
       releases before mutation). Documented at register_function.
    
    4. `ensure_ready` for register/unregister (mutators); `ensure_readable`
       for list_functions (read). Matches `register_format`/`add_sheet` vs
       `snapshot`/`cell`/`list_sheets`.
    
    5. No event emission v1 — function registration isn't a structural change
       in the §3.3 sense (sheet/table/name); contract §9 doesn't list it.
       Filed for 6.4-3 if observability demands it.
    
    6. No version-token bump on register/unregister — registry mutation does
       not advance `{epoch, op_count}` (workbook + op-log are untouched).
       Registry IS tooling, not document state.
    
    7. napi DTO `FunctionMetadataJson` mirrors `#[serde(rename_all =
       "snake_case")]` so wire bytes are stable if the IDE round-trips
       through JSON. `BigInt` for `FunctionImplHandle` (u64 — matches
       `OperationId` convention at `lib.rs:4351`).
    
    8. FaultGuard around the register/unregister mutation pair — matches the
       6.1C audit-fix M3 pattern for non-runtime mutators. Disarms cleanly on
       the legitimate-error Conflict path so the session does not seal on a
       `function_exists` / `function_not_found` user-level error.
    
