   268	    /// `rename_table`/`rename_column` (the only commands whose underlying
   269	    /// producer can emit MULTIPLE ops) wrap them in a single `Op::BatchCommit`
   270	    /// (`cells.rs` `set_value` `match log_ops.len()`; `sheets.rs`/`tables.rs`
   271	    /// rename producers append one `BatchCommit`), and `batch`/`commit_transaction`
   272	    /// append one `Op::BatchCommit`. So one `undo()` reverts exactly one command
   273	    /// with NO explicit `group_start`/`group_end` needed. (Proven by
   274	    /// `undo_clear_of_formula_cell_is_one_unit` + `undo_batch_reverts_whole_batch`.)
   275	    ///
   276	    /// **Re-materialization model.** Unlike `ql_collab::CollabSession` (snapshot-
   277	    /// cache + on_push cell-meta partial invalidation), `WorkbookSession` owns a
   278	    /// LIVE workbook and re-materializes it WHOLESALE after a Loro undo/redo:
   279	    /// `replay_into` the (now-compacted) op-log into a fresh workbook, rebuild
   280	    /// the calcgraph, recompute, then `bump_epoch`. So no on_push meta encoding
   281	    /// is wired here — kept deliberately simpler. See [`rematerialize`].
   282	    ///
   283	    /// **v1 depth cap (audit L1).** Loro's `UndoManager::new` defaults to
   284	    /// `set_max_undo_steps(100)`; the session does not override it, so the undo
   285	    /// stack silently drops the oldest unit past 100 undoable commands. Bounded
   286	    /// retention is intentional for v1 (mirrors the `ops`/`events`/`change_log`
   287	    /// bounds); a configurable depth is a later increment.
   288	    ///
   289	    /// [`rematerialize`]: WorkbookSession::rematerialize
   290	    undo_manager: loro::UndoManager,
   291	}
