#: F1
Sev: HIGH
Scope-item: extra / 7 / 12
File: `crates/ql-bindings-node/src/lib.rs:4522`
Finding: `function_metadata_from_json` forwards `canonical_name` unchecked, and `Session::register_function` then reaches `FunctionRegistry::register_metadata`, whose empty/lowercase canonical-name checks are `assert!`s at `crates/ql-functions/src/registry.rs:357` and `:361`. The binding docs explicitly say napi entrypoints do not use `catch_unwind` by default at `crates/ql-bindings-node/src/lib.rs:19`.
Why it matters: malformed JS input such as `canonicalName: "myudf"` or `""` can panic/abort instead of returning `[bad_argument]`. The docstring at `crates/ql-bindings-node/src/lib.rs:4747` also claims this is a `[bad_argument]` path, but it is not.
Suggested fix: validate or normalize `canonicalName` before constructing `FunctionMetadata`, returning `bad_argument_error`; preferably also make the core trait path return `EngineError::bad_argument` instead of relying on registry asserts. Add smoke tests for lowercase and empty names.

#: F2
Sev: HIGH
Scope-item: 2 / 12
File: `crates/ql-exec/src/session.rs:1069`
Finding: `open`/`import` preserve the old registry only after `*self = Self::from_workbook(...)` (`:1069-1071`, `:1142-1144`, `:1158-1160`). But `from_workbook` builds the calcgraph using a fresh default registry at `crates/ql-exec/src/session.rs:316-319`. The calcgraph rebuild API explicitly says UDF-aware callers must pass the session registry at `crates/ql-exec/src/calcgraph_session.rs:1137-1143`.
Why it matters: after registering UDF metadata, adopting a workbook can leave `self.registry` UDF-aware while `self.graph` was extracted against a UDF-free registry. Loaded formulas can have wrong/missing deps, arg-context behavior, or volatility tracking, causing stale incremental recalc later.
Suggested fix: add an adoption helper like `from_workbook_with_registry(workbook, Arc<FunctionRegistry>)` and rebuild the graph with that preserved registry before assigning the session. Add a test: register an Aggregate UDF, open/import/adopt a workbook containing `MYUDF(MyRange)`, then edit the range and prove the formula dirties.

#: F3
Sev: MED
Scope-item: 8 / 11 / 12
File: `crates/ql-bindings-node/src/lib.rs:4329`
Finding: `ArityJson` is not a strict tagged union. `arity_from_json` accepts `kind: "variadic"` while silently ignoring `n/min/max`, accepts `fixed` while ignoring `min/max`, and accepts `range` while ignoring `n` (`:4329-4360`). The docs say variadic “requires no payload” and mismatches surface `[bad_argument]` at `:4284-4286`.
Why it matters: bad JS DTOs are silently normalized instead of rejected, violating the No-Fallbacks rule and making JS→Rust→JS not faithful for malformed inputs.
Suggested fix: reject extraneous fields for each `kind`; also reject `range` with `max < min`. Add negative DTO tests.

#: F4
Sev: LOW
Scope-item: 8
File: `crates/ql-bindings-node/tests/smoke_session.mjs:158`
Finding: the implementation only supports omitted/undefined optional arity fields; the smoke comment documents that `null` triggers a generated napi `NumberExpected` before mapper validation. That means unbounded `Range { max: None }` is not accepted as both `max: null` and `max: undefined`.
Why it matters: if the JS contract expects `null` as a valid unbounded marker, callers get a non-engine-shaped conversion error instead of `[bad_argument]` or a successful `None`.
Suggested fix: either document “undefined/absent only” in the public DTO contract, or parse arity through a JS value/object layer that explicitly treats `null` and `undefined` as `None`.

#: F5
Sev: LOW
Scope-item: 9 / 10
File: `crates/ql-exec/src/session.rs:3368`
Finding: `register_function_dirties_dependent_formulas` does not actually prove dirty fanout or fn-gen rebind. It calls `recalc_dirty` and asserts `OperationState::Completed`, then asserts the cell is still an error (`:3368-3380`). But `run_recalc` marks operations completed regardless of whether anything was dirty (`crates/ql-exec/src/session.rs:870`), and empty dirty recompute is valid (`crates/ql-exec/src/workbook_runtime/recompute.rs:3217-3237`).
Why it matters: the H3 trait-wire test can pass even if `on_function_registered` never dirties anything and even if no trait-level fn_gen bump/cache miss occurs.
Suggested fix: in this unit test, inspect `s.graph` after registration and before recalc, or assert `s.registry.fn_generation()` bumped through the trait. Also prove re-extraction, for example by checking the formula node lands in `volatile_formulas` after registering a volatile UDF and recomputing.

